    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four dice:**\n\nEach die has 6 faces, so for four dice, the total number of possible outcomes is:\n\\[ 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 6^4 = 1296 \\]\n\n2. **Determine the number of favorable outcomes where all four dice show the same number:**\n\nSince there are only two numbers that all four dice can show (both numbers must be 1 or 2), there are:\n\\[ 2 \\times 6 = 12 \\]\nways for all four dice to show the same number.\n\n3. **Calculate the number of favorable outcomes where not all four dice show the same number:**\n\nThis is the total number of possible outcomes minus the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number:\n\\[ 1296 - 12 = 1284 \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the probability that all four dice show the same number:**\n\nThe probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes:\n\\[ \\frac{12}{1296} = \\frac{1}{108} \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the probability that not all four dice show the same number:**\n\nThis is the complement of the probability that all four dice show the same number:\n\\[ 1 - \\frac{1}{108} = \\frac{107}{108} \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{107}{108}} \\]",
